# TEMPLATE — the unit that RUNS fleet-sync.sh, driven by fleet-sync.timer beside it.
# Every ALL-CAPS placeholder has to be replaced before this file is installed. It carries no host,
# no address and no credential on purpose: this repository is public. The far side is named only by
# a git REMOTE in the follower's gitignored .git/config, and the key that reaches it lives in that
# user's ~/.ssh — the same rule fleet-sync.sh itself states in its head.
#
# IT HAS A TWIN NOW, AND THE TWIN IS NOT A FILE (W5d, owner 2026-09-23). Until W5d this pair was
# the FOLLOWER's only: under the standing decision (path b, 2026-09-05) the canonical host never
# fetched from the follower and never ran this script, so installing it there was a category error.
# Both hosts land now and the bare hub arbitrates, so both have to pull the other's work — and both
# pull it from the HUB, never from each other. The canonical host therefore runs the same script on
# the same schedule; what differs is two Environment= lines, not a second file:
#   Environment=FLEET_SYNC_REMOTE=hub        (on BOTH hosts — the hub is the only place to pull from)
#   Environment=FLEET_SYNC_BUILD_CMD=true    (canonical host ONLY — public/*.js there belongs to
#                                             POST /api/deploy, which builds before it restarts)
# A run with a non-default build command says so in its own line instead of claiming a bundle it
# did not produce, so the journal of the host that builds nothing stays readable.
#
# ONESHOT, AND ALLOWED TO FAIL LOUDLY — that is the whole design. fleet-sync.sh answers in seven
# exits (0 current or fast-forwarded · 2 fetch failed · 3 DIVERGED · 4 precondition refused · 5
# client build failed · 6 dependency install failed · 7 the deploy this host asked its own instance
# for could not be made), and anything but 0 leaves this unit `failed`, which is what
# `systemctl --user status fleet-sync` and `systemctl --user list-timers` then show. Four of them
# want a human. Exit 3 means this host HOLDS COMMITS THE HUB DOES NOT — since W5d landing here is
# allowed and a land that reached the hub can never cause this, so it is a direct commit that was
# never pushed, or a land whose push the hub refused, and the line names both. Exits 5,
# 6 and 7 all mean the fast-forward STANDS and something behind it does not: at 6 the dependencies
# (so the build was never attempted), at 5 the bundle — the board this host serves is then the old
# JS, or none at all, over new HTML — and at 7 the running srv, which keeps serving the code from
# before this sync. A 409 from the deploy route is NOT 7: it is the route saying "a land or an
# audit is in flight, ask again", the script prints it and exits 0, and the next timer run asks.
# Do NOT add `SuccessExitStatus=` to quieten any of them — a transport that fails silently is the
# failure this unit exists to make visible.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT GUARD, stated rather than implied. The tree it moves is the tree the follower's
# sessions and suites read, and this unit takes no lock — not the suite mutex at /tmp/fleet-e2e.lock
# and not git's. The exposure is smaller than it sounds: e2e-stage.sh copies a suite's tree into its
# own scratch directory before the suite runs, so what a fast-forward can land inside is that copy's
# own window, not a whole suite. It is not zero. That is why the timer beside this file is set in
# minutes rather than seconds, and why shortening it is a decision and not a tweak.
#
# NO [Install] SECTION, deliberately. This unit is started BY the timer; `systemctl --user enable`
# belongs on fleet-sync.timer alone. A service enabled by hand here would also run at boot, outside
# the schedule the timer states.
#
# Install (an owner act on the follower, after fleet-sync.sh has ARRIVED THERE THROUGH A LAND —
# an untracked hand-copied twin of a tracked file is exactly what makes the next ff-only sync fail):
#   sed "s|FLEET-DIR|$HOME/claude-fleet|g" fleet-sync.service > ~/.config/systemd/user/fleet-sync.service
#   sed "s|FLEET-DIR|$HOME/claude-fleet|g" fleet-sync.timer   > ~/.config/systemd/user/fleet-sync.timer
#   systemctl --user daemon-reload
#   systemctl --user start fleet-sync.service     # measure the fetch ONCE by hand before arming it
#   systemctl --user enable --now fleet-sync.timer
# `sudo loginctl enable-linger USER` is the same prerequisite fleet-watchdog.service names, and it
# is paid once for both: without it this user's whole systemd manager stops at logout.

[Unit]
Description=claude-fleet sync (fast-forward this checkout onto the remote's main; hub under W5d)
Documentation=file://FLEET-DIR/fleet-sync.sh

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=FLEET-DIR
ExecStart=/bin/sh FLEET-DIR/fleet-sync.sh

# EXPLICIT, for the reason its neighbour pays twice over: a service inherits systemd's own default
# PATH, not a login shell's. Most of what this one reaches is the distribution's — `git` and the
# `ssh` it shells out to — and for a long time that was the whole list, with a note here saying a
# home-relative entry would need a reason worth writing down next to it. THE REASON ARRIVED:
# fleet-sync.sh now builds the client bundle after a fast-forward (`bun run build`), because
# `public/*.js` is gitignored and a follower that only fetches serves HTML with no JS behind it.
# `bun` ships from no distribution and installs into `%h/.bun/bin`, so without this entry the build
# is a `command not found` from the timer that a terminal run can never reproduce. The script
# resolves `bun` itself as well, so an installed unit older than this line still builds — belt and
# braces on purpose, because only one of the two travels through a land.
Environment=PATH=%h/.bun/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# THE KEY IS NOT HERE AND MUST NEVER BE, the same sentence helper-daemon/fleet-helper.service makes
# about its token: an `Environment=` line is readable by every account on the box through
# `systemctl show` and /proc/<pid>/environ. ssh finds the follower's key through HOME, which the
# user manager already sets — and NOT through a forwarded agent, which exists only inside an
# interactive session. That is why the install above starts this unit once by hand: a fetch that
# only ever worked over a forwarded agent passes from a terminal and fails from the timer.

# The journal, not a file. watchdog.sh keeps its own server.log because it has prose of its own to
# write; this unit's whole output is one line from fleet-sync.sh, and `journalctl --user -u
# fleet-sync` keeps it with the exit status that explains it.
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
